<section class="numbered" id="specification">
  <h2><span class="secno">1</span>Specification</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>A tool version is described by one file, <code>recipes/&lt;tool&gt;/&lt;version&gt;/recipe.yaml</code>: its
      identity (name, version, licence, homepage, optional DOI and EDAM terms), the architectures it supports, how to
      obtain it and an acceptance test. The build type decides where packages come from:</p>
    <table class="booktabs">
      <thead><tr><th>Build type</th><th>Packages from</th><th>Lockfile</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">pixi-conda</td><td>conda-forge and Bioconda, solved by pixi</td><td class="mono">pixi.lock</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">uv-pypi</td><td>PyPI, solved by uv</td><td class="mono">requirements.lock</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">autotools · cmake · make</td><td>a release archive pinned by SHA-256, compiled in the builder stage</td><td>checksum in the recipe</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">rust-cargo</td><td>a source archive pinned by SHA-256, built with <code>cargo install --locked</code></td><td>checksum and <code>Cargo.lock</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">binary</td><td>a pre-built release archive pinned by SHA-256</td><td>checksum in the recipe</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Recipes are validated against a published <a href="{{ HUB_URL }}/tree/main/schema">JSON Schema</a> and by
      <code>capsid lint</code>. <a href="{{ ENGINE_URL }}/blob/main/docs/recipe-reference.md">Recipe reference →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="locking">
  <h2><span class="secno">2</span>Locking</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>The solver runs once, when the recipe is written, inside a pinned solver image, and its output is committed
      next to the recipe. The lockfile names each package by URL and SHA-256 for every target architecture. The image
      build installs exactly that list and does not consult the solver again; a checksum mismatch aborts it.
      Re-running a build months later therefore installs the same files.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="building">
  <h2><span class="secno">3</span>Building</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>Images are multi-stage builds. The builder stage installs the locked closure; the runtime stage starts from a
      Debian slim image referenced by digest, with system packages taken from a dated
      <a href="https://snapshot.debian.org/">snapshot.debian.org</a> archive, so <code>apt</code> resolves the same
      versions on every build. File timestamps in every layer are clamped to <code>SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH</code>, so a rebuild from the same
      recipe, lockfile and engine version is expected to yield the same image digest.
      <a href="{{ ENGINE_URL }}/blob/main/docs/reproducibility.md">Reproducibility guide →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="image">
  <h2><span class="secno">4</span>Image layout</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <table class="booktabs">
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">User</th><td>numeric, non-root <code>1000:1000</code>; works under Apptainer's user mapping and Kubernetes <code>runAsNonRoot</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Init</th><td><code>tini</code> as PID 1, forwarding signals and reaping children</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Tool tree</th><td>read-only for the runtime user</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>/etc/capsid/</code></th><td>the recipe, the lockfile, an SPDX SBOM, RO-Crate metadata, a Nextflow module and a Snakemake rule</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Labels</th><td>OCI labels for title, version, description, licence, upstream URL and source repository, plus <code>io.capsid.*</code> labels pointing to the files above</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><a href="{{ ENGINE_URL }}/blob/main/docs/image-design.md">Image design →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="publishing">
  <h2><span class="secno">5</span>Publishing</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <table class="booktabs">
      <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Workflow</th><th>Does</th><th>Permissions</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Pull request changes a recipe or stack</td><td class="mono">pr-audit</td><td>schema check, lint, build and test of each changed file</td><td>read-only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recipe submitted through the issue form</td><td class="mono">issue-submission</td><td>build and test in isolation; opens a pull request if it passes</td><td>build job read-only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Merge to <code>main</code></td><td class="mono">publish-release</td><td>build per architecture, test, push, sign, attest; pull the signed digest on native amd64 and arm64 and test again</td><td><code>packages: write</code>, <code>id-token: write</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fridays, 04:00 UTC</td><td class="mono">friday-robot-sync</td><td>proposes recipes for new Bioconda releases as a pull request</td><td>opens pull requests only</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The workflows install the Capsid engine from a fixed commit, so a change to the engine cannot alter a
      published image without a reviewed change to this repository.
      <a href="{{ ENGINE_URL }}/blob/main/docs/security.md">Security model →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="updates">
  <h2><span class="secno">6</span>Updates</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>A published version is not rebuilt with new tool or library versions; those arrive as a new recipe version.
      An existing version may be republished after the engine's weekly update of base-image and snapshot pins, to pick
      up security fixes in the runtime layer. That moves the tag, never an existing digest. Pipelines that must be
      exactly repeatable should reference digests.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="limits">
  <h2><span class="secno">7</span>Limits</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>The lockfile fixes what is installed, not what the upstream package build contains; a compromised upstream
        artefact would be locked faithfully. Signatures establish where an image was built, not that its sources are sound.</li>
      <li>Digests on this site are resolved when the site is compiled; the registry remains the authority.</li>
      <li>PyPI lockfiles do not record licences; for <code>uv-pypi</code> images the licence column defers to PyPI and the SBOM.</li>
      <li>Identical digests on rebuild are the design target and can be checked with the procedure in the
        <a href="{{ ENGINE_URL }}/blob/main/docs/reproducibility.md#verifying-a-rebuild">reproducibility guide</a>. Source builds can
        still differ in bytes when a build tool ignores <code>SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH</code> or embeds paths or host names.</li>
      <li>An artefact deleted upstream (a yanked release, a moved URL) makes a recipe unbuildable; the published
        images remain available, and their embedded lockfile and SBOM still document what was used.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
